Date: Wed, 08 Dec 93 01:38:47 EST
From: wit@MIT.EDU (paul whitmore)
Subject: Personality above intelligence

-
--
---
One of the essential organizing principles in my research is to pursue 
ideas ad hominem. When someone writes a single argument of interest to 
me, it seems more helpful to read as much of their previous work as 
possible, rather than pursue topical references to related research. 
Repeatedly, after I became excited by a single paper, further study of 
the writer's work rewarded my inquiry in many unexpected ways.

"Reputation," rather than being an explanation, simply codifies this 
mystery. The question is, how are knowledge structures organized, since 
personalities seem to be the most concise method of giving shape to the 
problem space? 
AI (including decision theory, machine learning, knowledge 
representation) has not even begun to touch on the artifice of 
personality. Even though someone might assume that personality only 
matters in squishy domains such as philosophy and the social sciences, 
think of these minds: Feynman vs Gell-Mann, Maxwell vs Gibbs, Boltzmann 
vs. Prigogine.  The ideas uncovered, the formulation of their 
discoveries, and the coherence of their perspective, attract me to read 
some of these guys, and to find others impenetrable. Constructive 
insights spring from the person, rather than the intelligence. Nietzsche 
once wrote "The system is dead but the personality behind it is 
irrefutable and immortal. Plato for instance." 
If we view personalities as a mysterious and unexplored structure, there 
is no reason to think that humans will be replaced by bigger brains. I 
don't believe that personalities are "unexplainable," the last refuge 
for an elan vital. I would say that each personality is probably no more 
complex an informational structure than an animal in an ecosystem. But 
each individual personality might be a species unto itself, only as much 
like another as one mammal resembles another. ( vague metaphor) 

one clue that makes it hard to grasp the level of depth in personality 
structure: children emerge, around the age of two, with a complete, 
unique, integral personality. but this just reinforces my belief that 
the most important thing about our genetic heritage is coded in our 
personality structures, rather than in our intellectual problem-solving 
abilities. 

