From: wit@MIT.EDU (paul whitmore)
Subject: AIT-Life Itself-

-
--
---
when binary oppositions come up at so many levels, does this 
indicate something about either our minds or the world? In physics, 
e.g., contrast the laws with the initial conditions. Or in Algorithmic 
Information Theory, granting the freedom to choose axioms, distinguish 
the postulated from the deduced.
The recurrent theme, to put it coarsely, is that One embeds the Many. 
Instances generate a rule, and if one operates at the level of the rule, 
rather than enumerating the particular basis for the rule, one ascends 
to a level of description that is far more powerful. What if the 
justification for the rule, which is treated as a closed deterministic 
deductive formalism, happens to be probabilistic? Then, for the price of 
occasionally being wrong, one can get all the right descriptions in one 
fell swoop.
Robert Rosen describes, in  Life Itself, a completely novel approach 
that places biology, and a developed theory of organization, in a place 
that embeds physics as a trivial case, where mechanistic explanations 
are viewed as degenerate systems, the easy integer, rather than the far 
more abundant and complicated Real. One suggestion is that a biological 
system generates bits, some pieces of physical matter, and then treats 
those things in a way not reducible to their probabilistic physicality. 
The bits are embedded in a systematic relationship, treated as 
symbolically standing in relation to something for future use. This 
referencing act, generated randomly, results in the bits being defined 
functionally. From the frame of the cell, the bits are determined from  
top-down, as being embedded in a symbolization, an identity, rather than 
a probability.  
Rosen argues rigorously that this symbolic relation cannot be modelled 
in syntactic, mechanistic, computational theories. (using category theory rather than a string of adjectives) He has created an 
incompleteness argument against current physical theories. How do 
biological systems elude the limits of mechanistic computation? By 
randomly searching the solution space, they have moved along the 
sequence of Chaitin's omega, nibbling away without knowing what is 
happening. But each mutation, each generation, does represent a decision 
branching through the space of all statements. Although not all 
statements are computable within a given formalism, they can be reached 
nondeterministically. Once you find a bit in Omega, it doesn't matter 
how you got it. It can now be used, as a Godelian sentence, to ascend 
beyond the formalism in which the system is embedded.
So it is possible to view biological systems as random generators, which 
use the Godelian sentences as leveraging them outside the particular 
limit of their previous computational power. This trick, which looks 
like the decision to encode information, can only be understood at a 
level of interpretation beyond the level at which the encoding occurs. 
DNA can only be understood from the stance of the cell, and then the 
cell becomes embedded in a higher cardinal power of computation, and 
this continues, as the embedding randomly arises up to organisms, in 
ecosystems, in economies, in linguistic communities, in scientific 
industries. Each can treat the next level down as determinate, and 
operate upon it, even though, given the flux of quantum mechanics and 
thermodynamics, it is not as fixed as it is treated to be for purposes 
of symbolization. 
Robin Hanson posted to AltInst a mechanism that suggested one way to 
move from probabilistic descriptions to closed one or zero perspectives. 
Imagine some connectionist auction occurring at a neural level, where 
the highest bidder gets, not a probability, but an assigned value of 
certainty:
---begin quote-----
"[some] combinations of items are especially valuable to certain folks, 
and less than this whole combination is much less valuable.  
For example, to move your oil from A to B, you
need a connected series of pipes from one end to the other.  This
results in "non-convexities" in demand for these items, so that
standard economics theorems implying the existence and optimality of
competitive equilibria do not apply.  Translated: selling these items
one at a time doesn't work very well, in theory or in practice.  ....
economists have worked to find better ways to directly auction 
off these sort of assets.  I'll call these alternatives "bundle 
auctions". ...
 [ J. Banks, J. Ledyard, D. Porter, "Allocating uncertain and
  unresponsive resources: an experimental approach" RAND J. Econ. 20(1)
  Spring 1989.]
Our basic bundle auction is called AUSM, and is very simple.  Each bid
puts a price on a specific bundle of resources.  At each moment in the
auction the resources are allocated to some non-conflicting set of
bundle bids "on the board".  If you come in with a new bid for a new
bundle, it probably conflicts with some bids on the board, wanting
resources allocated to them.  The rule is that your bid gets to bump
these bids if the price you offer with your bid is more than the sum
of the prices for the bids yours conflicts with.  That is, your new
bid gets on the board only if it can raise the total price offered for
all the items.  Once your bid gets on the board, you can't retract it,
though it may get bumped.
---end quote-----
A paper recently posted for the Brain and Behavioral Sciences* re-opened 
old dualistic antinomies. Their review of experimental psychology 
concludes that there are two distinct mechanisms for learning, 
instance-based and rule-learning. This supports the sense that basic 
processes of human thinking take advantage of some recurrent dichotomy.  
Why think that the depth of the duality extends beyond human 
computational processes? 
I don't know where to take this right now. It's cranky to buy into any 
duality. If we considered its pervasiveness, we might try to describe 
thing from within a frame and its antinomy (maybe this just refers to 
Bohr's complementarity, or Joni Mitchell's Look at Life from Both Sides 
now ) 
