Date: Wed, 8 Dec 1993 9:48:07 +0000 (GMT)
From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
Subject: META: Decline and Fall of the Extropians Li

Edward J OConnell <ejo@world.std.com> writes:

>My friend Glen Grant, a Canadian SF writer and editor, pointed out that 
>Charlie Stross sells to interzone regularly. I was impressed! Way to go!

Hey, I'm flattered that someone noticed ... but not recently. (I've
only finished one short story in the past year.) Reason? Well, there's
a novel which should be out sooner or later -- but more importantly,
the pay's rubbish and the audience is dwindling. If I write an article
for, say, Computer Shopper, it gets a circulation (in the UK) of
120,000 and pays UK pounds 140/1000 words. A story in Interzone gets a
circulation about 7-9000 and pays UK pounds 30/1000 words. It's also
easier to sell magazine features than stories.

The picture changes when you get into books, but it's still not enough
to live on. Indeed, the fiction industry has been chasing its tail
down the plug-hole for years. Too many people want to write, not
enough people want to read, and what they _do_ want to read seems to
be trite brain candy. 

I maintain that anybody who's serious about writing fiction these days
probably isn't doing it for a living unless they started selling years
ago (when the situation was rosy). For example: M. John Harrison, a
very literary British SF author. First novel, The Committed Men, came
out in about 1970 and had a hardback print run of 12,000 in the UK
(plus >100,000 in paperback). Today you'd be lucky to see a paperback
print run of 10,000! Yet more new books are published today than ever
before. 

Something is wrong with the publishing industry. It's in a spiral of
lowering standards, increasing breadth, and decreasing profitability.
The smart money is on finding a new creative outlet of equivalent
durability, artistic merit, and superior profitability (on which to
live and create more art, etcetera). 

Anyone got any ideas for media which can be worked in with minimal
investment by a solitary specialist? I'm all ears.



