        :
>For example, in this country the behavior of many blacks is pointing to
>their _increased_ "marginalization." A community of hoodlums,
>"gangstas," rappin' bums, basketball wannabees, and welfare addicts.
>Malcolm X must be spinning in his Dewar by now.

Very probably he is. Note however the appearance of an affluent black middle
class community. Perchance the two phenomena are related?

A royal commission in the UK recently intensely annoyed the government
(which is strong on law'n'order, it likes to think) by reporting that
there is no correlation between broken/one-parent families and crime:
the correlation is between relative poverty and crime. Note the
_relative_ in there. If you're subsisting on a dole that's twice the 
wage of a professional in another country it doesn't make a blind bit 
of difference, because that dole's still only 10% of the working wage
and you're surrounded by people more affluent than yourself. To a
certain mind-set the logical way out of this quandry is not to get a
job but to go steal something.

As the gap between rich and poor widens, the crime rate goes up. This
can be seen as an argument for taxing the rich; more sensibly, as an
argument for figuring out way to let the poor better themselves more
easily. Anyone got any suggestions (of the latter kind)?

        :
>Nancy asks about the dwindling number of women at the Hackers Conference:

>> Any theories about why the number is decreasing? This sounds like 
>> something other than biological hard-wiring.

>I've been going for the past six of these, and each year there are
>fewer women. This year, only 6 women, out of an attendance of about
>220.

Here's a hypothesis. You may not like it.

Computing -- at least in the UK -- was a surprisingly female-friendly
profession up until 20 years ago (so I'm told). Up to 30% of computing
staff were women, and although management tended to be male dominated
(as elsewhere in UK industry then and now) it was still a fairly
acceptable career path.

However, since the microcomputer boom, the number of women going into
computing as a career has fallen by 50%. Why?

At a guess, gender role-modelling has something to do with it.

Until about 1975, computers were not accessible to the general public;
there were no gender-specific role models associated with work in the
field, and no social criteria to apply to the job. As far as most
people were concerned, computer programmers might as well have been
martians or actuaries. It was a clean job that didn't require
mechanical strength, though, so there was no overt 'weak female'
discrimination. 

But schools in the UK began computerizing in about 1980. Typically
each school would get a handful of primitive micros that would be
shared among entire classes; only in the late 80's did schools have
enough machines for everyone to get to one.

In coeducational classes, boys tend to be more aggressive and get a
significantly larger proportion of teacher attention (if for no other
reason than because if they're left alone they disrupt the other kids'
attention). They're also more likely to be interested in the kind of
games that were then available on the computers (with an emphasis on
violence -- attitudes to which are culturally dictated at an early
age). So the boys tended to hog the computers, preventing girls from
getting access to them.

(This is a generalized description of some educational surveys that
have been published over the years. I only read features based on the
originals, and can't give references. Any educators on the list?)

In addition to being deprived of on-line time, the girls were
therefore conditioned to respond to computers as boys toys, at the age
(11-16) when they were most vulnerable to social gender role
specification: puberty. By the time they were old enough to make
sensible decisions about their future career they were already locked
into different career paths.

This is a positive-feedback loop. As computers are seen as more of a
"male" preserve, so females are deterred from working with them.

Please don't tell me that women have no engineering aptitude. My
girlfriend is a HyperTalk hacker and wants to get into C in her spare
time. Another of her friends has bust a gut to get onto a mech. eng.
course where she can monkey around with racing cars. Anecdotal, I know
-- like my brother-in-law the Philosophy professor, who lives in
America and happens to be black -- but if you collect enough anecdotes
together you wind up with a statistically significant hypothesis.

Another friend of mine enrolled on a C programming course at night
school; she wanted to learn quite badly, but was the only woman in a
class of twenty. After six weeks, during which time the instructor
apparently picked on her, she dropped out. Why? Well, it might boil
down to a different challenge response. We're conditioned from an
early age to behave differently, depending on gender; boys are trained
to attempt to outface a challenge, while girls are trained to be
submissive. Being submissive in a dominance-assertion situation (such
as that between a sexist teacher and a student) is liable to
exacerbate the problem. (Ever been in a situation of walking away from
a fight, _knowing_ that if you break into a run they'll come after
you?)

>That's another major difference between men and women, of course.
>Women always want more emotion. (This Danish chick I was mentioning
>was constantly saying to me: "Timothy, tell me what's _real_ for you!"
>When I told her, she just repeated this mantra.)

>I find the Extropians discussions more than adequately filled with
>emotion. Women, I think, are just more interested in hearing about
>emotional reactions....part of evolving in the cave around other women
>and babies and needing to be sensitive to feelings, warning signals of
>distress, etc.

Not evolution: training. I know women who go gooey over the mere sight
of a baby, and women who's instant reaction is to turn on the oven and
start looking for some stuffing and seasoning. I know women who
display all the emotional response of a carrot and men who burst into
tears and confide their heart's leaning at the drop of a hat. It's
worth noting that a lot of the examples Tim is coming out with  match
popular stereotypes, and it's probably even more illuminating to
consider that the non-standard responses I've mentioned belong to
people who explicitly, usually consciously, _reject_ social
programming: that is, to deliberate non-conformists.

I don't believe the human species has been around long enough for
genetic selection pressure to change our fundamental personality
traits along sexual lines. 

However, the theory of memes _does_ explain the gender differences
bloody well -- there's an entire complex of low-level memes associated
with social/emotional interaction that we assimilate unconsciously at
an early age, and most of us never question them. They're so pervasive
that they give the impression of being genetic in origin. And they're
pernicious and totally anti-extropian in orientation. All of us, male,
female, and in between, are hosts for these low-level parasites.

A question for the list: what would be the implications, role-wise,
for a civilization in which nanotechnology-based medicine makes
possible substantial cosmetic surgery and actual physical
sex-switching (down to the chromosomal level in every cell)? (See 
_Steel Beach_ by John Varley for one approach ...)



